  "worked_solution": "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{cm}$ with $\\text{m}.$ To get rid of $\\text{cm},$ we can put $\\text{cm}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{m},$ we can put $\\text{m}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{cm}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The speed given in the problem statement is $972\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}.$\n\n- There are $100$ centimeters per meter. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\frac{1}{100}$ meters per centimeter.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}972\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}} & =972\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{cm}} \\\\  & =972\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}) \\\\  & =9.72\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the speed of the cruise is $9.72\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$",
